  "analysis": [
    { "label": "The set-up", "text": "A free kick around 35 metres out is normally a crossing position. France set a four-man wall and Barthez stood just off centre, ready for a cross or a shot aimed over the wall." },
    { "label": "The strike", "text": "After a very long run-up, Roberto Carlos hit through the ball with the outside of the left foot. Striking it off-centre at that pace loaded it with sidespin and sent it on a line well wide of the wall and, seemingly, the goal." },
    { "label": "The swerve", "text": "As the ball slowed in flight, the sidespin took over and bent it sharply back towards goal. The curve came so late that the ball clipped the inside of the post and went in." },
    { "label": "Why it worked", "text": "Barthez read the early flight as going wide and never moved. With the bend arriving that late, a keeper set for a normal free kick had no time to react." }
  ],
